{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-ddbc1d78-80ba-411f-ad50-959973fd2d03-130","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→//! Time travel: periodic snapshots plus the input of every tick. Rewinding restores the nearest\\n//! earlier snapshot and re-simulates forward with the recorded inputs.\\n\\nuse std::collections::VecDeque;\\n\\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\\n\\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\\nuse crate::sim::SimState;\\n10→\\npub struct History {\\n    pub snapshots: VecDeque<SimState>,\\n    /// (tick, input used to advance from `tick` to `tick + 1`)\\n    pub inputs: VecDeque<(u64, InputFrame)>,\\n    /// Ticks between snapshots.\\n    pub interval: u64,\\n    /// How far back rewind can go (ticks).\\n    pub window: u64,\\n    pub enabled: bool,\\n20→}\\n\\nimpl Default for History {\\n    fn default() -> Self {\\n        Self { snapshots: VecDeque::new(), inputs: VecDeque::new(), interval: 20, window: 60 * 30, enabled: true }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nimpl History {\\n    pub fn clear(&mut self) {\\n30→        self.snapshots.clear();\\n        self.inputs.clear();\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Call before stepping from `state.tick` with `input`.\\n    pub fn record(&mut self, state: &SimState, input: &InputFrame) {\\n        if !self.enabled {\\n            return;\\n        }\\n        if self.snapshots.back().is_none_or(|s| state.tick >= s.tick + self.interval) {\\n40→            self.snapshots.push_back(state.clone());\\n        }\\n        self.inputs.push_back((state.tick, *input));\\n        while self.snapshots.len() > 1 && self.snapshots[1].tick + self.window <= state.tick {\\n            self.snapshots.pop_front();\\n        }\\n        let oldest = self.snapshots.front().map(|s| s.tick).unwrap_or(0);\\n        while self.inputs.front().is_some_and(|(t, _)| *t < oldest) {\\n            self.inputs.pop_front();\\n        }\\n50→    }\",\"content_concise\":\"1→//! Time travel: periodic snapshots plus the input of every tick. Rewinding restores the nearest\\n//! earlier snapshot and re-simulates forward with the recorded inputs.\\n\\nuse std::collections::VecDeque;\\n\\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\\n\\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\\nuse crate::sim::SimState;\\n10→\\npub struct History {\\n    pub snapshots: VecDeque<SimState>,\\n    /// (tick, input used to advance from `tick` to `tick + 1`)\\n    pub inputs: VecDeque<(u64, InputFrame)>,\\n    /// Ticks between snapshots.\\n    pub interval: u64,\\n    /// How far back rewind can go (ticks).\\n    pub window: u64,\\n    pub enabled: bool,\\n20→}\\n\\nimpl Default for History {\\n    fn default() -> Self {\\n        Self { snapshots: VecDeque::new(), inputs: VecDeque::new(), interval: 20, window: 60 * 30, enabled: true }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nimpl History {\\n    pub fn clear(&mut self) {\\n30→        self.snapshots.clear();\\n        self.inputs.clear();\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Call before stepping from `state.tick` with `input`.\\n    pub fn record(&mut self, state: &SimState, input: &InputFrame) {\\n        if !self.enabled {\\n            return;\\n        }\\n        if self.snapshots.back().is_none_or(|s| state.tick >= s.tick + self.interval) {\\n40→            self.snapshots.push_back(state.clone());\\n        }\\n        self.inputs.push_back((state.tick, *input));\\n        while self.snapshots.len() > 1 && self.snapshots[1].tick + self.window <= state.tick {\\n            self.snapshots.pop_front();\\n        }\\n        let oldest = self.snapshots.front().map(|s| s.tick).unwrap_or(0);\\n        while self.inputs.front().is_some_and(|(t, _)| *t < oldest) {\\n            self.inputs.pop_front();\\n        }\\n50→    }\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-222132__x35112d/work/Wobble Minutes-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132/engine/crates/pav_core/src/history.rs\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"//! Time travel: periodic snapshots plus the input of every tick. Rewinding restores the nearest\\n//! earlier snapshot and re-simulates forward with the recorded inputs.\\n\\nuse std::collections::VecDeque;\\n\\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\\n\\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\\nuse crate::sim::SimState;\\n\\npub struct History {\\n    pub snapshots: VecDeque<SimState>,\\n    /// (tick, input used to advance from `tick` to `tick + 1`)\\n    pub inputs: VecDeque<(u64, InputFrame)>,\\n    /// Ticks between snapshots.\\n    pub interval: u64,\\n    /// How far back rewind can go (ticks).\\n    pub window: u64,\\n    pub enabled: bool,\\n}\\n\\nimpl Default for History {\\n    fn default() -> Self {\\n        Self { snapshots: VecDeque::new(), inputs: VecDeque::new(), interval: 20, window: 60 * 30, enabled: true }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nimpl History {\\n    pub fn clear(&mut self) {\\n        self.snapshots.clear();\\n        self.inputs.clear();\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Call before stepping from `state.tick` with `input`.\\n    pub fn record(&mut self, state: &SimState, input: &InputFrame) {\\n        if !self.enabled {\\n            return;\\n        }\\n        if self.snapshots.back().is_none_or(|s| state.tick >= s.tick + self.interval) {\\n            self.snapshots.push_back(state.clone());\\n        }\\n        self.inputs.push_back((state.tick, *input));\\n        while self.snapshots.len() > 1 && self.snapshots[1].tick + self.window <= state.tick {\\n            self.snapshots.pop_front();\\n        }\\n        let oldest = self.snapshots.front().map(|s| s.tick).unwrap_or(0);\\n        while self.inputs.front().is_some_and(|(t, _)| *t < oldest) {\\n            self.inputs.pop_front();\\n        }\\n    }\\n\",\"total_lines\":109}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce","uuid":"5b092b2b-547e-4037-9737-e75ea64346f2"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_67","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Calculating snapshot intervals: 1800 ticks equals 90 snapshots across a 30-second window.\n\nFull SimState snapshots would be too heavy for parallel tests. History is set to 8 seconds to keep snapshot overhead manageable. InputFrame must be Copy for history storage.\n\nUiEvent must be Copy to keep InputFrame Copy. The enum will use u16 for hover, click, and slider values. Device is a u8.\n\nGameCmd is Copy, so the new command field fits the InputFrame layout. Button names will be added to the global registry for name-based lookup. Engine patches will be applied first, followed by the main game implementation. Bot menu navigation will use a `BotWant` enum with a `Press(button)` variant.\n\nBotWant will also include Lean and Wait variants. The bot converts these wants into InputFrame presses, pulsing them only when the want changes or on a fixed interval. Continuous confirm spam is acceptable because the bot re-evaluates each tick after every screen change. Name selection will confirm immediately if focus starts at the desired name.\n\nThe bot will press direction buttons until focus reaches the target item, then confirm once. Focus updates in the same tick as the direction press, so the next tick aligns correctly. Focus is stored as a u16 on the shell. The schedule carousel lists today's events plus an End Day option.\n\nThe bot will select the first unplayed event, or End Day if the goal is already met. Intro dialogue advances on confirm while the item list is empty. The age slot machine is cosmetic: a 1.5-second roll that the bot can skip with confirm. Pip comments are chosen by change and worst metric from the script. Event IDs will be defined as a const string slice.\n\nThe event roster is finalized with twelve activities. Unlocking follows a five-day progression: breath and pose on day one, squat and plank on day two, step floatie and lap on day three, tide paddle tray and cannon on day four, and big stand after the check on day five. Free play mode opens all events immediately.\n\nMinutes are banked as one plus stars for first-time completions, or one otherwise. Star ratings derive from score thresholds: 850 points for three stars, 650 for two, 400 for one, and zero below that.\n\nPlank star ratings are now defined by time thresholds: 55 seconds for three stars, 40 for two, 25 for one, and below that for zero. Titles will be selected from a score-based index of thirty entries. The \"Yesterday You\" effect will use a tinted ghost NPC entity spawned in the simulation frame rather than appending render objects directly.\n\nHub ghosts will be tinted via PuppetFrame in App::frame before build. Local ghosts will use pale colors on a second character entity, which is visible in captures without engine tint support.\n\nThe ghost tinting approach is being refined. Instead of adding a tint map to the engine, the bot will modify object tints directly in `sim.frame` after objects are built. It will scan the object list and apply the fit game's tint for any entity that matches a ghost identifier.\n\nLocal ghosts will be tinted by mutating the `puppet.tint` field on each matching object in `sim.frame`. Hub ghosts are not entities; they will be appended as extra `RenderObjects` to cloned previous and current frames in `app.rs` before `builder.build`, since the scene is built from those already-built frames.\n\nGhost visuals will be built from JSON PuppetFrame data. A pure `ghost_visible` function will test that ghosts match by area. The Play Together podium test will drive the bot through two players and three short events, totaling roughly eighteen thousand ticks. Medley events may serve as the actual events. The test harness must run the bot in each seat.\n\nThe bot will cycle through each seat in the Play Together podium. After a result it confirms the pass card and advances to the next seat. The test ends when the flow reaches Podium.\n\nMedley scores will be deterministic because both simulations use the same seed and input sequence. The test runs both bots to medley completion and compares their final scores.\n\nIn tests the hub flag is set immediately so the medley starts right away. In the browser hub it waits for HUB_GO.\n\nThe browser hub will set `boot.hub = true` and `boot.scene = fit/medley` only when `AIHub.active` is true. The Rust side reads this flag to decide whether to wait for `HUB_GO` or start the medley immediately.\n\nThe `hub_session` flag will be set from `__gameBoot.hub`, which JavaScript enables only when `AIHub.active` is true. In tests this flag is false.\n\nWhen a hub session is active, the pause menu will show only Resume and Settings. Quit and dev options are hidden. The world does not freeze on pause because the hub clock keeps running.\n\nThe `frozen` method returns false for hub sessions so the world never freezes. During a hub pause, the event simulation continues but player input is ignored. Hub pause does not stop input and sound; that only happens when the hub ends.\n\nDuring a hub pause the world will keep running but gameplay input will be zeroed so the player cannot control while the menu is open. The clock and background effects like gusts will still tick.\n\nFor silent attract mode the app will only play music events when `audio_resumed` is true. This prevents attract audio from starting until the user actually unlocks it.\n\nMusic events will still fire during attract mode even though audio is not started. The Mixer will gain a `render_bus` method so tests can compare bus-level output at 0.10 versus full volume.\n\nMusic gain will be tested by rendering the same song at 0.10 and full volume. A quiet patch will be used so tanh stays nearly linear, and the RMS ratio will be checked against a 15% tolerance. At least thirty SFX IDs will each produce non-silent audio for a short render, with peak levels verified above zero. Two speakers will use different formants or pitches.\n\nVoice synthesis will use syllable events with speaker, vowel, and pitch. Formant filters will be added to Patch as optional fields. I am checking how many Patch literals omit the `..Patch::new()` shorthand so I can add defaults correctly.\n\nPatch will gain `f1`, `f2`, and a `formant` flag defaulting to zero. When the flag is active, the voice synthesizer applies two peaking bandpass filters to shape each syllable's spectrum. Tests will verify that different speakers produce distinct audio by comparing RMS levels or waveform correlation.\n\nA compact step sequencer will run inside Mixer::render so songs advance sample-accurately. Songs will be static data, and a Music event starts playback. The app will read an AtomicU64 song position to phase-lock audio across native and wasm. Tests will verify non-silent output by rendering one second of music.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-4ad29d68-f6d8-4fee-9446-ff535c0ca3ad-131","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"fn spawn_npc","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-222132__x35112d/work/Wobble Minutes-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132/engine/crates/pav_core/src/sim.rs","-A":8}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":627,"output_tokens":3572,"cache_read_input_tokens":194688,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce","uuid":"7dd44baf-e1b7-48ca-90d9-9db782d728b2"}
