{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-9b1de6bb-aee9-4aec-a0bc-5f58cdab2956-99","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"390→            corner_radius,\\n            fill_color,\\n            stroke,\\n            stroke_kind,\\n        ))\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn rect_filled(\\n        &self,\\n        rect: Rect,\\n400→        corner_radius: impl Into<CornerRadius>,\\n        fill_color: impl Into<Color32>,\\n    ) -> ShapeIdx {\\n        self.add(RectShape::filled(rect, corner_radius, fill_color))\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn rect_stroke(\\n        &self,\\n        rect: Rect,\\n        corner_radius: impl Into<CornerRadius>,\\n410→        stroke: impl Into<Stroke>,\\n        stroke_kind: StrokeKind,\\n    ) -> ShapeIdx {\\n        self.add(RectShape::stroke(rect, corner_radius, stroke, stroke_kind))\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Show an arrow starting at `origin` and going in the direction of `vec`, with the length `vec.length()`.\\n    pub fn arrow(&self, origin: Pos2, vec: Vec2, stroke: impl Into<Stroke>) {\\n        use crate::emath::Rot2;\\n        let rot = Rot2::from_angle(core::f32::consts::TAU / 10.0);\",\"content_concise\":\"390→            corner_radius,\\n            fill_color,\\n            stroke,\\n            stroke_kind,\\n        ))\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn rect_filled(\\n        &self,\\n        rect: Rect,\\n400→        corner_radius: impl Into<CornerRadius>,\\n        fill_color: impl Into<Color32>,\\n    ) -> ShapeIdx {\\n        self.add(RectShape::filled(rect, corner_radius, fill_color))\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn rect_stroke(\\n        &self,\\n        rect: Rect,\\n        corner_radius: impl Into<CornerRadius>,\\n410→        stroke: impl Into<Stroke>,\\n        stroke_kind: StrokeKind,\\n    ) -> ShapeIdx {\\n        self.add(RectShape::stroke(rect, corner_radius, stroke, stroke_kind))\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Show an arrow starting at `origin` and going in the direction of `vec`, with the length `vec.length()`.\\n    pub fn arrow(&self, origin: Pos2, vec: Vec2, stroke: impl Into<Stroke>) {\\n        use crate::emath::Rot2;\\n        let rot = Rot2::from_angle(core::f32::consts::TAU / 10.0);\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/egui-0.36.2/src/painter.rs\",\"offset\":390,\"limit\":30,\"raw_output\":\"            corner_radius,\\n            fill_color,\\n            stroke,\\n            stroke_kind,\\n        ))\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn rect_filled(\\n        &self,\\n        rect: Rect,\\n        corner_radius: impl Into<CornerRadius>,\\n        fill_color: impl Into<Color32>,\\n    ) -> ShapeIdx {\\n        self.add(RectShape::filled(rect, corner_radius, fill_color))\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn rect_stroke(\\n        &self,\\n        rect: Rect,\\n        corner_radius: impl Into<CornerRadius>,\\n        stroke: impl Into<Stroke>,\\n        stroke_kind: StrokeKind,\\n    ) -> ShapeIdx {\\n        self.add(RectShape::stroke(rect, corner_radius, stroke, stroke_kind))\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Show an arrow starting at `origin` and going in the direction of `vec`, with the length `vec.length()`.\\n    pub fn arrow(&self, origin: Pos2, vec: Vec2, stroke: impl Into<Stroke>) {\\n        use crate::emath::Rot2;\\n        let rot = Rot2::from_angle(core::f32::consts::TAU / 10.0);\\n\",\"total_lines\":569}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce","uuid":"69667d26-c26b-401e-868b-8f5e8e0d8755"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_281","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"CornerRadius accepts f32 values through the Into trait.\n\nStrokeKind::Outside is available in egui. Context and screen dimensions are accessible during run_ui. Font registration is handled by install_fonts.\n\ninstall_fonts registers Fredoka and Nunito from static font bytes. It maps both names to their respective font families and promotes Nunito as the proportional default. The resulting definitions are applied via ctx.set_fonts.\n\nFont paths resolve correctly from the fit module. Empty titles are skipped. Large numbers such as scores and countdowns render at maximum size. A top-right DEMO ribbon appears during demo mode. The footer draws the prompt word from FitGame::footer.\n\nThe glyph and action are drawn separately to guarantee visibility. The title footer reads \"Cross\" for device 0. A `type_px` helper scales font sizes proportionally from a 1920x1080 baseline, ensuring both the title and body text remain readable at that resolution. Headless output must contain \"Wobble Minutes\", \"New Stay\", and \"Cross\".\n\nStepping the simulation with device 2 updates the internal device state before rendering. The glyph therefore reflects the new device, and the resulting frame captures the correct symbol.\n\nAudio tests will be added to pav_audio. A test will verify that Sfx id 1 is non-empty. The music render test carefully checks that ten percent gain produces exactly one-tenth the amplitude of full gain.\n\nThe tanh audio curve scales linearly enough for the amplitude ratio test. A sample scaled by 0.1 gain produces roughly one-tenth the sum of absolute values compared to full gain, so the 0.02 tolerance passes.\n\nThe music render test now uses a continuous sine wave at 0.2 amplitude instead of a sparse envelope. A beat envelope is added to prevent a pure tone, but it still scales linearly with gain so the amplitude ratio test remains valid. Every song from 1 to 7 is checked to ensure it produces non-silent output.\n\nA peak amplitude check ensures every song stays above 0.01. All 32 SFX IDs are validated with a 0.3-second render, each requiring a peak above 0.001. The syllable test will verify that speaker 0 and speaker 1 produce different audio vectors.\n\nThe explosion test still passes because song 0 and the voice path were not modified. SimEvent must implement Clone for tests. I will write the remaining files now, update the status tick, and add a `tests/run.sh` script with notes. In app.rs I need to import UiEvent from pav_core::input and use pav_core::input::buttons. The Fit sample will include mouse lean.\n\nMouse lean is calculated from cursor offset relative to screen center with the Y axis flipped. When the mouse has not moved, the device keeps its last lean value instead of switching back to keyboard input. The key ramp always updates from held directions and switches `lean_from` to 2 when keys are pressed. The pad stick uses a shaped stick function with a 0.12 deadzone and 1.5 sensitivity.\n\nThe pad stick is active when its length exceeds 0.12, setting `lean_from` to 1. The last input device that moved in the current frame wins: mouse beats pad and keys, keys beat pad, and pad beats nothing. If no device moved this frame, the previous lean state is preserved.\n\nThe mouse travel counter will be computed directly in the CursorMoved handler rather than through a new Input method. This keeps the change minimal and avoids altering every cursor update site.\n\nA `note_pointer` method will accumulate cursor travel for mouse lean. The travel counter resets at the end of `fit_sample`. App code will detect clicks by checking `left_tap` or `touch_at`, then pass a `clicked` flag into `fit_sample`.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-78636083-d8d7-4e38-9a9a-88baed24552b-100","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"guide_visible|card_visible","glob":"**/pav_app/src/app.rs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":348,"output_tokens":2702,"cache_read_input_tokens":165120,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce","uuid":"70b986b7-93bf-4c39-ae03-b890f7095871"}
