97,99,116,32,115,116,97,114,116,115,32,97,110,100,32,97,110,32,105,110,112,117,116,32,101,120,105,116,115,32,105,116,46,32,80,104,111,110,101,32,97,110,100,32,116,97,98,108,101,116,32,67,104,114,111,109,105,117,109,32,112,114,111,102,105,108,101,115,32,115,104,111,119,32,226,128,156,67,111,110,116,114,111,108,108,101,114,32,111,114,32,107,101,121,98,111,97,114,100,32,114,101,113,117,105,114,101,100,226,128,157,44,32,107,101,101,112,32,97,116,116,114,97,99,116,32,114,117,110,110,105,110,103,44,32,97,110,100,32,97,32,83,101,116,116,105,110,103,115,32,116,97,112,32,111,112,101,110,115,32,83,101,116,116,105,110,103,115,46,32,87,101,98,75,105,116,32,119,105,116,104,32,97,110,32,105,80,104,111,110,101,32,112,114,111,102,105,108,101,32,40,110,111,32,87,101,98,71,80,85,32,104,101,114,101,41,32,115,104,111,119,115,32,116,104,101,32,102,97,108,108,98,97,99,107,32,116,104,97,116,32,98,111,116,104,32,97,32,99,111,110,116,114,111,108,108,101,114,32,111,114,32,107,101,121,98,111,97,114,100,32,97,110,100,32,87,101,98,71,80,85,32,97,114,101,32,114,101,113,117,105,114,101,100,46,32,84,104,101,32,100,114,97,119,105,110,103,32,115,117,114,102,97,99,101,226,128,153,115,32,100,105,109,101,110,115,105,111,110,115,32,101,113,117,97,108,32,116,104,101,32,114,101,113,117,101,115,116,101,100,32,118,105,101,119,112,111,114,116,46,32,65,32,98,108,97,110,107,32,32,91,46,46,46,32,116,114,117,110,99,97,116,101,100,32,40,49,48,51,52,32,99,104,97,114,115,32,116,111,116,97,108,41,93,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":1,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-222132__x35112d%2Fwork/01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce/goal/plan.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":15,\"content\":\"2. **gating:** Launch the shipped HTML on its real entry path more than once. Prefer `file://`. If a server is required, bind `127.0.0.1` with `python3 -u -m http.server 0` and stop only that PID. Use Playwright Chromium (already installed) with the WebGPU flags in TASK.md. At 320×180, twice: zero page errors, `__gameStatus` appears and its tick advances across samples, a standard-mapping gamepad stub moves menu focus and confirms, keyboard does the same, mouse hover moves focus and click activates using rects the page publishes, prompts change with the device, Settings exposes Xbox glyphs, and after about 10 real seconds attract starts and an input exits it. Phone and tablet Chromium profiles show “Controller or keyboard required”, keep attract running, and a Settings tap opens Settings. WebKit with an iPhone profile (no WebGPU here) shows the fallback that both a controller or keyboard and WebGPU are required. The drawing surface’s dimensions equal the requested viewport. A blank Web [... truncated (1031 chars total)]\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce","uuid":"a30a4abf-8959-4e78-b55c-acc5a4222356"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_580","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"BUTTONS array index 13 corresponds to BTN_DPAD_DOWN.\n\nThe pad flake is likely a timing issue. The test will hold the button and poll until focus changes, up to two seconds. For Xbox, it will move, wait for hover on the glyph, click, and poll for up to one second. A real bug may exist: mouse clicks could be consumed by egui over the game_lines popup, or `over_ui` might drop the tap if egui claims pointer input on the settings screen.\n\nThe settings title click succeeded. The glyph may be obscured by something. Painter-only UI should not trigger egui pointer input. The root panel might be the culprit.\n\nThe central panel in egui 0.36 likely does not intercept clicks by default. The iPad crash during Page.evaluate is probably an out-of-memory error from the large canvas at 810x1080 forced to device scale factor 1.\n\nThe iPad crash likely stems from 20GB of shared memory being exhausted by the large software WebGPU canvas. The iPhone 13 test probably passed because its smaller viewport avoids this. The three passing tests may be the iPhone and WebKit runs. The captures step was never reached because the iPad exception killed the process before it could execute.\n\nCaptures ran successfully and passed. The three passing tests are iPhone, WebKit, and captures. The three failures are boot1, boot2, and iPad. I should verify the generated PNG files exist.\n\nThe iPad out-of-memory crash is caught inside the browser block, so the suite continues to captures after closing the browser. I will inspect the generated PNG files to confirm they are valid. To reduce iPad memory pressure, I should close contexts promptly.\n\nThe iPad crash may be a browser-level WebGPU failure on the oversized surface rather than an OOM kill. I will try a smaller viewport while keeping the iPad device profile identity.\n\nA separate browser instance should handle touch profiles to prevent memory from accumulating across runs. The iPad viewport can be reduced further without breaking the test, since the touch card and settings tap should work at any size. Disabling dev-shm and limiting JavaScript heap size will not help because the crash is caused by WebGPU SwiftShader memory pressure.\n\nI will check available memory with `free -h` before changing the iPad viewport. The iPad test waits up to 45 seconds for attract mode, during which WebGPU renders a large frame every animation frame and may exhaust memory. I am considering a 600×800 or 768×1024 viewport override while keeping the iPad device profile identity.\n\nThe iPad crash likely stems from a GPU memory leak across the prior boots, phone, and iPad runs. The phone survived 45 seconds of attract mode without issue, so the iPad failure is probably not just viewport size. A separate browser instance for touch tests should isolate this leak.\n\nI will add a single retry if the page closes during the iPad test. The crash occurs inside `status(page)` when `page.evaluate` runs after the attract wait loop, confirming a browser-level failure rather than a simple OOM kill.\n\nI will hold the pad button until focus changes, then hover over the glyph before clicking. A fresh browser instance will be launched for touch profiles, and a separate one for iPad. I will check available memory before deciding whether to reduce the iPad viewport or device scale factor. After the browser tests pass, I will run the hub tests. I will also inspect the generated capture PNGs.\n\nI will check the game code if the Xbox glyph click still fails after the hover-then-click fix. The pad hold-until-focus-change logic looks correct. The keyboard test will wait for ArrowDown to move focus from 1 to 2 and prompt for Space. I verified that `poll_gamepad` sets `last_device = Gamepad` on any activity, and that MENU_DOWN increments focus only once even when held across multiple frames.\n\nThe slow event loop could miss a button release if the poll interval is too long, but holding until focus changes prevents this. If the game still never sees the press, the test failure will reveal a real bug to fix. The DPadDown mapping from gilrs is trusted because boot 1 already passed. Chromium flags `--disable-dev-shm-usage` and `--renderer-process-limit=1` will be added to reduce iPad memory pressure.\n\nThe touch profile does not verify that the drawing surface matches the device viewport. The iPad crash is caused by the full device viewport size, so shrinking it would violate the tablet profile identity. The plan requires the tablet Chromium profile to show Controller or keyboard required, keep attract running, and open Settings on tap.\n\nI will try the native iPad viewport once in a fresh browser. If it still crashes, I will retry with a smaller viewport and note this as a deviation. The test must keep the iPad device profile identity, so the user agent, touch support, and mobile flags remain unchanged.\n\nThe prior agent already capped the iPad device scale factor at 1 to avoid software WebGPU framebuffer allocation failures. If that still exhausts memory, capping the CSS viewport is the same class of fix. I will check the iPad Gen 7 viewport size and available memory, then read the PLAN verification requirements for hub tests. The next steps are updating browser.py, checking captures and memory in parallel, and running the browser tests. I may also add a `__fitClick` fallback for the Xbox glyph click.\n\nThe plan requires real mouse clicks on rects the page publishes, so `__fitClick` is insufficient. Hover-then-click is the correct approach. At 320×180 viewport there is no scroll, so coordinate bugs are unlikely. The `xbox` boolean is top-level in JSON and copies correctly during publish.\n\nA missed click on the Xbox glyph would leave the setting unchanged, since only CONFIRM activates it and PRIMARY is stripped when a UI event exists. This supports the theory that the glyph click is failing to register.\n\nA `click_item` helper will move the mouse, wait for the hover label, click, and wait for the effect. This prevents the mouse from hitting the wrong row after opening settings or pressing back. I will check hub.py and PROGRESS.md only after the browser tests pass.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-30a14ba4-5fb9-473d-8a28-b018ffb9e676-45","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"free -h; echo '---'; ls -la \"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-222132__x35112d/tmp/grok-goal-b3947fd4d9d9/implementer\"/capture-*.png 2>/dev/null; echo '---'; python3 -c \"import playwright; from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright; p=sync_playwright().start(); d=p.devices['iPad (gen 7)']; print({k:d[k] for k in d if k in ('viewport','device_scale_factor','is_mobile','has_touch','user_agent')}); ip=p.devices['iPhone 13']; print('iphone', ip['viewport'], ip['device_scale_factor']); p.stop()\"","description":"Check memory, captures, device viewports"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1900,"output_tokens":3044,"cache_read_input_tokens":80128,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce","uuid":"84365a3a-e595-496e-887b-28257f34a487"}
