{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_601","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The tick failed to advance on both boots.\n\nThe clock froze at tick 5 again. The Wait fix should still be present. Recent changes to consumed logic and over_ui are unlikely to be the cause. A stale test page is now suspected.\n\nStale build is ruled out. The window_event change might be causing an infinite loop or blocking redraw. The consumed flag change is unlikely to blame because the tick check runs before pad input. Other suspects include run.sh launching differently, memory pressure, or the timer flags failing again.\n\nThe freeze is now confirmed as the original rAF cancellation bug. The Wait switch in about_to_wait must be checked. run.sh might not pass the browser flags correctly. Headless throttling is unlikely because both boots failed deterministically. The rebuild may have broken the Wait path.\n\nfinish_init may be failing silently. If ready is false, Wait is never set and the loop reverts to Poll, which cancels rAF and freezes the tick at 5. The status display still works because it comes from frames.\n\nThe about_to_wait logic is being examined. It sets ControlFlow::Wait when ready, then requests a redraw. The previous fix relied on this to schedule one rAF per frame. If something reverts the control flow back to Poll, the loop cancels rAF again and the tick freezes at 5.\n\nThe exact tick 5 freeze matches the bug signature before the Wait fix. The current about_to_wait and run() functions need inspection for accidental control flow changes. A cached WASM file is unlikely because each test launch uses a fresh browser and the HTML was just rewritten. The remaining suspects are that the new build lacks the Wait switch, the switch fails to trigger, or the headless flags were not applied in this run.\n\nPhone, iPad, WebKit, and captures all passed. The two failures are only the 320×180 boots. This points to a viewport-specific or initialization-order issue rather than a global bug.\n\nThe boots returned False before the attract and pad tests, so those desktop inputs were never actually run. The touch-screen frame cap does not explain the desktop freeze.\n\nThe 320×180 desktop context is the only one that fails. The previous HTML at this size advanced ticks successfully, but the new build freezes at tick 5. This suggests the regression is specific to the current code rather than a viewport or headless throttling issue.\n\nA size-specific panic is unlikely. The test returns early on tick failure before checking page errors, so a hidden crash could be the real cause. The touch tests may have passed for an unrelated reason. The next step is to inspect the Python touch attract check.\n\nThe touch attract check is not the cause. The 320×180 viewport is now suspected of being treated as invisible in headless mode, which would throttle rAF and freeze the tick.\n\nMemory pressure is unlikely because the tick froze at exactly 5, meaning zero frames ran in 400ms. A stall would still advance some ticks. The headless flags should prevent throttling at this size. The next step is to read run.sh and verify browser.py receives the correct launch flags.\n\nI am now searching app.rs for any code that resets the control flow back to Poll. I also want to verify whether another poll source is still cancelling the redraw request even with Wait enabled. The build and test ran in separate sequential commands, so a stale page load is not the cause.\n\nThe HTML path is identical between build and test, so a wrong file is not the cause. Chromium file:// caching was also ruled out: if the old page were loaded from cache, the tick would advance normally, but it still freezes at 5. The regression is therefore in the new code itself.\n\nI will re-read about_to_wait, the window_event edit, and the frame input section to check for accidental damage. I also suspect egui might panic on a 320×180 viewport after a few frames, which would stop redraws and freeze the tick.\n\nThe two recent changes only affect event handling and input sampling, not the frame loop itself. They should not freeze the clock unless they trigger a panic after tick 5. A panic could occur from cursor events during boot or from double-handling once the fit UI appears. I will capture page errors to verify this.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-c9516f33-8f0c-4956-b230-dc1bbca4e6e7-79","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"set_control_flow|ControlFlow::","glob":"**/pav_app/src/app.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-c9516f33-8f0c-4956-b230-dc1bbca4e6e7-80","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-222132__x35112d/work/Wobble Minutes-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132/tests/run.sh","limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":328,"output_tokens":2344,"cache_read_input_tokens":120192,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce","uuid":"7f4f7190-3b58-49b3-8395-1b287a5ccdd8"}
