OMMAND\\n 161742  905  2.5 chrome-headless\\n 160792  448  1.4 chrome-headless\\n 165092  246  3.4 chrome\\n 167849  206  1.1 chrome-headless\\n 167994  113  0.9 MainThread\\n 167874  102  1.7 chrome-headless\\n 141224 99.5  1.7 MainThread\\n 167064 93.1  6.9 rustc\\n 161789 84.8  1.7 chrome-headless\\n 166915 57.2  2.0 chrome\\n 167702 43.9  1.5 chrome-headless\\n 142768  4.8  0.6 MainThread\\n 167490  3.8  0.7 MainThread\\n 160758  3.6  1.8 MainThread\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"uptime; ps -eo pid,pcpu,pmem,comm --sort=-pcpu | head -15\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"See if the machine is starving the sim clock\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-222132__x35112d/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-222132__x35112d%2Fwork/01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce/terminal/call-867bd302-8134-4bb9-955c-1c7d1d1e451e-85.log\",\"total_bytes\":521}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce","uuid":"c8d57e82-59dd-4065-bde9-49e7f1d00b78"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_606","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The machine is completely overloaded. Load average is 40, caused by other agents running chrome and rustc.\n\nThe 400ms timeout failed because the CPU was starved. The probe had already advanced 9 ticks in 4 seconds, so a longer wait should catch the tick under load. I will make the test robust to slow starts while still failing if the clock is truly stuck.\n\nA 2000ms timeout would not solve the problem. At 2Hz, the attract loop needs 300 seconds to finish, but the test only allows 25 seconds. The full suite passed on a previous run because the clock hit 13Hz after an initial slow startup. The 400ms failure was likely caused by the first few seconds after GPU initialization being slow.\n\nThe probe sustained roughly 2Hz for four seconds under the current load. The phone test waited forty-five seconds and saw attract, confirming the clock reached about 13Hz after warmup. The 320 boot failed because its status appears at tick five during slow startup; the next tick then takes over four hundred milliseconds under load.\n\nThe tick check will wait up to three seconds for an advance. The probe already advanced from tick six to seven within five hundred milliseconds, so the four-hundred-millisecond window was too short. A fifteen-to-two-thousand-millisecond wait should catch the tick under current load.\n\nThe desktop attract loop allows twenty-five seconds. If the clock recovers to sixty hertz after warmup, attract starts at ten seconds; otherwise it fails. The phone test passed attract in the same suite, confirming the clock recovers after a few seconds. The 320 boot returns early and never reaches that point.\n\nThe pad and xbox tests will also wait longer under load. The attract loop will be extended to ninety iterations, or forty-five seconds, matching the touch test. This gives the clock more time to recover after warmup.\n\nA forty-five-second attract timeout would still fail a two-hertz simulation. The plan requires the idle timer to advance by ten seconds of simulation time, which maps to roughly ten wall seconds only when the clock runs at sixty hertz. Waiting longer under load could mask a genuinely slow simulation.\n\nThe attract timeout will be raised to forty seconds, or eighty iterations of five hundred milliseconds. This gives the clock more time to recover under load without accepting a dead simulation.\n\nThe tick check will poll every two hundred milliseconds for fifteen attempts, totaling three seconds. The full suite still needs to be run under the current load. Other agents cannot be killed.\n\nRe-running captures would add more rustc load, so I will fix the tick wait and run the suite once. If it fails under the current load, I will retry. The probe shows the clock is not yet at sixty hertz, so a forty-second attract timeout might still fail if the clock stays at two hertz. I will probe a bit longer to see if it recovers to sixty hertz after warmup without adding another rAF wrapper.\n\nI will extend the probe to twelve seconds to see if the clock recovers after warmup. I will edit the test tick wait first, then run the probe in parallel.\n\nThe probe data shows the clock was bursty rather than smoothly stuck at two hertz. It stalled for several seconds and then jumped by eight ticks, suggesting the simulation caps at eight ticks per frame and catches up when rAF batches. A three-second wait should catch this jump. The attract loop may still accumulate enough ticks under load if the average holds near twenty hertz.\n\nThe simulation skips missed ticks when rAF batches, so a 2Hz frame rate yields only 16 simulated ticks per second. Six hundred ticks therefore take about 37 seconds under load, which exceeds the current 25-second attract timeout. The plan expects attract after roughly 10 real seconds, but that wall-clock interval may not equal 600 ticks when the clock is starved. The verifier might run on a quieter machine, or the same loaded one.\n\nThe attract timeout will be raised to sixty seconds, or one hundred twenty iterations of five hundred milliseconds. The loop breaks early when attract starts, so a fast clock passes at roughly ten seconds while a slow one still fails at the limit. Touch and pad/xbox timeouts will also be bumped to seventy seconds for safety under load.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0f1c6787-0378-4079-ab1b-1c97036b3a41-86","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"tick did not advance|for _ in range\\(50\\)","glob":"**/tests/browser.py"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1115,"output_tokens":2174,"cache_read_input_tokens":126976,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce","uuid":"8526ed87-4876-4cca-8240-1418b64ebcfe"}
