ay the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:21:32\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-222132__x35112d/work/TASK.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":150,\"raw_output\":\"SOURCE: Wii Fit\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall/blob/main/START_HERE.md, making a new game on its engine. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\nScope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:21:32\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\nTesting Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n\",\"total_lines\":151}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce","uuid":"d4a8dcce-a920-4510-a898-6c04c9e5adba"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_1006","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I will review the plan acceptance criteria and check prior code gaps.I need to review PLAN.md acceptance criteria and the current code for the 8 prior gaps.","signature":"n13iSF9Bq316THyd35AVsAcVZzp8MODtJKOOIRbEzIKzHKVh7kAEbX9Si13BvuNy72bpDAVdTeMiaeiL0s9/FddIaoex/R0+J1q5IN+W0Yrx5TiqpftnOG5MM/M6u85E0nfhgoOVuWMZ4lai2gZL7AtQL5du/OJsLHKg/1SPq1uDizdE45/t9S98VbrjdZL5pLNI8J13CVGX0JDEl7xwuRXamU3o/R4rW5lfeKs"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0e63656b-9649-4d25-9188-32fb0810cd03-3","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-222132__x35112d%2Fwork/01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce/goal/plan.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0e63656b-9649-4d25-9188-32fb0810cd03-4","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"UiEvent::Slider|fn fit_sample|time_scale|Press-to-skip|press.to.skip|eyes.closed|take\\(6\\)|take\\(4\\)|page","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-222132__x35112d/work/Wobble Minutes-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132/engine/crates","glob":"**/*fit*"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0e63656b-9649-4d25-9188-32fb0810cd03-5","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"slider|time_scale|dev_mode|schedule|final_age|eyes_closed|outfit|pose|beach","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-222132__x35112d/work/Wobble Minutes-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132/engine/crates/pav_core","glob":"**/*fit*"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4446,"output_tokens":367,"cache_read_input_tokens":24320,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce","uuid":"d6501f95-86ae-4413-a513-f7e7b11d1271"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-0e63656b-9649-4d25-9188-32fb0810cd03-3","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# Plan: Ship a playable Tippy Cay balance-and-wellness demo inspired by Wii Fit, plus the hand-in files TASK.md names\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-in package.** The work-folder root contains `PROGRESS.md`, a parseable `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape TASK.md specifies (every value filled, `\\\"usage\\\"` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.genre` one of the allowed catalogue genres, `aiHub.players` `1–4`), and one self-contained HTML file named `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132.html`. A sibling folder with that same basename (no `.html`) holds the dev tree. `{Title}` is a playful riff on “Wii Fit”, chosen by listing 50 titles and selecting one with a real RNG; the same string is the title-screen name, the page `<title>`, and `aiHub.name`. The 50 titles and the RNG draw are recorded in the dev folder. Opening the HTML from disk boots this game (a WebGPU-missing message only when WebGPU is absent).\\n2. **Wii Fit loop, original world.** It is a third-person sports mini-game anthology with a light life-sim frame: a five-day stay at Tippy Cay staffed by talking gym gear. The only play verb is a centre-of-pressure lean (left stick, keyboard ramp, or mouse offset from screen centre — last device wins) driving one shared balance model: shaped lean, stiffness/damping, sim-time wobble, tip only after the dot stays outside the unit disc for the tip time. Tips cost time or score and never end the stay. Each morning a wobble check (still, lean targets, eyes-closed tone, step timing) yields a Balance Age from PLAN.md’s skill formula, clamped 18–99; better input gives a lower age. There is no weight, BMI, body-shape, or diet content, and no Nintendo product names inside play (About source line and the title riff only). Days open in order — stillness, strength, cardio, lagoon — with minute goals 3, 5, 7, 9, then a Day-5 Big Stand whose last phase is 30 seconds of doing nothing (fake “press to skip”; any press forfeits the bonus). First clear of an event banks 1 + stars; repeats bank 1. Age 40 or better may check out by rowboat; otherwise the Extended Stay ending. Both roll credits and unlock free play. A headless bot, from a fresh New Stay, reaches credits on 3 seeds: won, 5 stamps, every event played, under 60 sim-minutes, without reading the wall clock.\\n3. **Every event is a finished mini-game.** Shipped and directly startable: Morning Wobble Check, Breath Bloom, Pose Parade, Tempo Squats, Plank Standoff, Step Party, Floatie Spin, Morning Lap, Tide Table, Paddle Slalom, Tray Trek, Cannonball Cup, and the five-phase Big Stand, with the twists named in PLAN.md §5 (breath guide, drifting pose ring and gull, squat tempo, plank flop, 96 BPM two-lane chart, hula-style spin and ring catches, lap with step-rate zone, shell-tilt boards, paddle gates, tray of smoothies, cannonball judges, Long Stillness). Each event has a card, countdown, result (score, stars, deadpan title, minutes, personal best, Retry or Back), a bot that earns at least 2 stars on 5 seeds, and an idle-input result under 1 star. Score events use 0–1000 with stars at 400, 650, and 850; Plank uses the time gates in PLAN.md (25 / 40 / 55 s). Time-cut permission in PLAN.md §17 does not apply: this computer can run the full roster.\\n10→4. **10-foot play shell, audio, and dev tools.** Same screens for pad, keyboard, and mouse. Prompts follow the last input: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs via Settings, keycaps, or mouse buttons. Bindings match PLAN.md §3, including pause and schedule. After 10 seconds (600 sim ticks) with no input on the title or pre-game menus, attract mode loops the PLAN.md §12 sequence with a DEMO ribbon; any input returns to the title; nothing is saved; it stays silent until a real input. Pause freezes the world and opens Dev Mode (not in a hub session) with jump, cheats, time scale, live sliders for the tunables named in PLAN.md §11, and reset to defaults. Settings include music default 0.10 linear, SFX, voices, glyphs, lean sensitivity, wobble assist, rhythm offset, text speed, fullscreen, and reset-save confirm. Touch-only devices get a “Controller or keyboard required” card plus Settings, no touch controls, and attract keeps running. All music, at least 30 sound effects, and Animalese/Simlish voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); speakers are audibly distinct; dialogue types out with syllable blips. UI is large enough for 10 feet (body ≥34 px and titles ≥64 px at 1080p, scaled down with the frame), at most two lines of dialogue, no dense stat tables. Look is high-key pastel on the engine’s cel style, with new beach and wellness props and new exercise poses; library props only for generic dressing.\\n5. **Saves, local multiplayer, and AI Hub.** Versioned `localStorage` saves cover up to four guest slots (name from a preset list, look, day, minutes, stars, bests, ghosts, ages, flip-flops, unlocks) and global settings, written on day change and on each result. Real dates passed in at boot drive absence comments; the sim does not read the clock. Play Together is hot-seat for 2–4 guests through the same three-event medley, with a pass-the-pad card and a podium. Yesterday You replays a best run beside you from day 3 and does not affect scoring. An AI Hub session in mode `ghost`, 1–4 players, skips title, menu, attract, quit, restart, and dev mode, and opens into the Inter-Resort Medley (Paddle Slalom, Tide Table, Cannonball Cup, same seed, same course). Other seats are tinted ghosts in a matching area only. Final score is the sum (0–3000) reported to the hub. Solo play leaves `AIHub.active` false. The About/Credits screen shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback, plus font and motion-library credits. No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features remain.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. **gating:** Add in-repo tests that construct the shipped game from a fresh start (no browser, no GPU) and drive its real update loop. Assert criterion 2’s bot run (3 seeds), criterion 3’s per-event bot and idle results (5 seeds), balance-model settle and tip timing, age direction and 18–99 bounds, attract start at 600 idle ticks plus exit without saving, pause freezing world time, music default 0.10, a dev slider changing a live value and resetting, save round-trip, medley determinism for the same seed and inputs, and Play Together reaching a podium. Lint shipped text for `TODO`, placeholder copy, weight/BMI/diet talk, and Nintendo names outside the About source line. Put them behind one command that prints only failures and a final `N passed, 0 failed` line. Run that command twice; both runs must print the same pass line. Save the log to `{SCRATCH}/tests-core.log`.\\n2. **gating:** Launch the shipped HTML on its real entry path more than once. Prefer `file://`. If a server is required, bind `127.0.0.1` with `python3 -u -m http.server 0` and stop only that PID. Use Playwright Chromium (already installed) with the WebGPU flags in TASK.md. At 320×180, twice: zero page errors, `__gameStatus` appears and its tick advances across samples, a standard-mapping gamepad stub moves menu focus and confirms, keyboard does the same, mouse hover moves focus and click activates using rects the page publishes, prompts change with the device, Settings exposes Xbox glyphs, and after about 10 real seconds attract starts and an input exits it. Phone and tablet Chromium profiles show “Controller or keyboard required”, keep attract running, and a Settings tap opens Settings. WebKit with an iPhone profile (no WebGPU here) shows the fallback that both a controller or keyboard and WebGPU are required. The drawing surface’s dimensions equal the requested viewport. A blank WebGPU canvas while ticks advance is the known software-readback limit, not a pass: also capture a 1920×1080 frame with the engine’s headless capture of the title (menu and title text readable) and of one event (figure plus balance puck substantially filling the frame). A blank engine capture is a failure. Two browser boots and two engine captures must agree. Logs and images go to `{SCRATCH}/`. If Playwright or the engine capture cannot start, save that launcher error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log`; criteria 2–4 then rest on step 1 plus the static checks in step 4. A capture that runs and returns a blank frame is not this fallback.\\n3. **gating:** Hub check on the shipped page. Serve the page with the hub harness files and open the harness for 2 players; for 3 and 4 players with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; and for `scenario=host-leave`. Each run must finish with the harness complete flag and its own pass flag set. Then open the page outside a session, press Enter and Space, and require no errors, `AIHub.active` false, and `Math.random` left unseeded. The session client embedded in the page must byte-match the downloaded `session.js`. Save logs to `{SCRATCH}/tests-hub.log`. If the hub files cannot be fetched, keep this step gating and record the blocker in `{SCRATCH}/hub-unverifiable.log` instead of inventing a pass.\\n4. **gating:** Static hand-in check. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object with the TASK.md keys; `usage` is `added on import`; `aiHub.name` equals the title screen. The HTML filename and dev-folder name match criterion 1. The page’s about-data block includes Source Information and the three feedback lists. `PROGRESS.md` exists. Dev-folder notes contain the 50 titles and the RNG output. Confirm no audio files are referenced by the game.\\n5. **evidence:** One 1920×1080 engine capture per set, plus a short filmstrip of at least one hand-keyed pose. Sound: either the unit render at music 0.10 is about a tenth of full scale, or a browser patch of the audio buffer shows non-silent music after a gesture. Absence of these images does not fail the goal when steps 1–4 pass.\\n\\n20→## Non-goals\\n- Not a Wii Fit recreation: no BMI or weight goals, no Miis or face editors, no Wuhu Island, no source music, layouts, or quoted lines, and not the full 40-activity list (soccer heading, ski jump, hula hoop as named in the source, and the rest).\\n- No real Balance Board, DualSense, iPhone, TV, listener, or second human. Use the stand-ins in TASK.md.\\n- No touch-play controls, no shared-world netcode, no FPS optimization, and no review subagents.\\n- No audio files and no spoken real words.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work root: `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json`, and `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132.html`.\\n- Dev folder of that basename: clone of `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall` (PLAN.md was checked against commit `49ef2ba`), game content and tests, notes, and a small README for adding an event, a set, or a song.\\n- AI Hub client files from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` (`session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`).\\n30→- Fredoka and Nunito (SIL OFL) embedded for UI text; engine puppet, egui, and synthesized audio extended as PLAN.md §§8–14 describe. Behavior is the contract; file names in PLAN.md are guidance.\\n- Playwright 1.63 (system Python, not `python3 -I`), Cargo, and `wasm-bindgen` 0.2.129. Chromium only for WebGPU play; Firefox and WebKit are fallback-message checks.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md for design numbers, writing, and the hub snippet; do not invent a second balance model or a second synth. Keep the whole flow (menus, dialogue, pause, settings, dev toggles, events) in the simulation so tests drive it with no GPU. Keep UI a pure view of that state. Keep tunable numbers and all dialogue in data files the tests load. Build a single HTML only after the sim tests pass. Disable the engine’s demo keys and on-screen chrome while this game is active so captures show the game, not the engine shell. Record any spec gap in one line in the dev notes and continue.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [x] Pick the title with a real RNG from 50 riffs, record the draw, create the dev folder, clone the engine, and keep `PROGRESS.md` current.\\n- [x] Register the game, hide engine chrome, add lean input and the title/pause shell, save, and the hub page tags so a boot smoke test passes.\\n- [x] Implement the shared balance model, the morning check, the island day loop, and one full event; lock them with sim tests.\\n40→- [x] Implement the remaining events, day unlocks, Big Stand, both endings, free play, and the bot playthrough.\\n- [x] Add synthesized music, effects, and gibberish voices (music default 0.10), attract mode, settings, dev sliders with reset, and the touch-only card.\\n- [x] Add wardrobe, flip-flops, Yesterday You, Play Together, and the hub ghost medley; run the hub harness.\\n- [x] Write the full script and About text, fill `exit-interview.json`, build the single HTML into the work root, run the one test command twice, capture title and one event, then delete build caches only.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n- Splash rounds call `AIHub.ready()` when `connect` resolves. Waiting for `__gameStatus` left the fourth software-WebGPU copy past the harness's 20s ready gate. A later `ready()` is a no-op.\\n- Big Stand uses music bed 3 and the credits use bed 6, matching the sim's song map rather than the plan's bed numbers. Long Stillness is song 0, which stops the bed.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n50→- PLAN.md §17 allows cutting Tray Trek, Floatie Spin, and Plank Standoff if time runs short. The objective requires everything this computer can run, so a cut roster fails criterion 3.\\n- TASK.md warns that a headless WebGPU screenshot can be blank even when the frame is live. A blank browser bitmap is not success and not an automatic environment waiver: the engine’s own capture must show a filled frame (step 2). Only a launcher that will not start is the fallback.\\n- Browser timing at 1920×1080 is too slow here (software WebGPU). The 10-second attract check is valid only around 320×180; a slow 1080p browser clock is not a game bug. Judge the 10-foot layout from the 1080p engine capture.\\n- Firefox and WebKit have no WebGPU here, so they cannot play the game. There is no physical pad, touch screen, listener, second human, or TV.\\n- A fresh clone may not be commit `49ef2ba`. If a “verified” engine note is wrong on the cloned revision, adapt and record the commit and the change; do not pretend the old note still held.\\n- Other agents share this machine. Use one browser, bind an OS-picked localhost port, and stop only PIDs this run started. `earlyoom` killing the browser is memory pressure, not a game bug.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# Plan: Ship a playable Tippy Cay balance-and-wellness demo inspired by Wii Fit, plus the hand-in files TASK.md names\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-in package.** The work-folder root contains `PROGRESS.md`, a parseable `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape TASK.md specifies (every value filled, `\\\"usage\\\"` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.genre` one of the allowed catalogue genres, `aiHub.players` `1–4`), and one self-contained HTML file named `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132.html`. A sibling folder with that same basename (no `.html`) holds the dev tree. `{Title}` is a playful riff on “Wii Fit”, chosen by listing 50 titles and selecting one with a real RNG; the same string is the title-screen name, the page `<title>`, and `aiHub.name`. The 50 titles and the RNG draw are recorded in the dev folder. Opening the HTML from disk boots this game (a WebGPU-missing message only when WebGPU is absent).\\n2. **Wii Fit loop, original world.** It is a third-person sports mini-game anthology with a light life-sim frame: a five-day stay at Tippy Cay staffed by talking gym gear. The only play verb is a centre-of-pressure lean (left stick, keyboard ramp, or mouse offset from screen centre — last device wins) driving one shared balance model: shaped lean, stiffness/damping, sim-time wobble, tip only after the dot stays outside the unit disc for the tip time. Tips cost time or score and never end the stay. Each morning a wobble check (still, lean targets, eyes-closed tone, step timing) yields a Balance Age from PLAN.md’s skill formula, clamped 18–99; better input gives a lower age. There is no weight, BMI, body-shape, or diet content, and no Nintendo product names inside play (About source line and the title riff only). Days open in order — stillness, strength, cardio, lagoon — with minute goals 3, 5, 7, 9, then a Day-5 Big Stand whose last phase is 30 seconds of doing nothing (fake “press to skip”; any press forfeits the bonus). First clear of an event banks 1 + stars; repeats bank 1. Age 40 or better may check out by rowboat; otherwise the Extended Stay ending. Both roll credits and unlock free play. A headless bot, from a fresh New Stay, reaches credits on 3 seeds: won, 5 stamps, every event played, under 60 sim-minutes, without reading the wall clock.\\n3. **Every event is a finished mini-game.** Shipped and directly startable: Morning Wobble Check, Breath Bloom, Pose Parade, Tempo Squats, Plank Standoff, Step Party, Floatie Spin, Morning Lap, Tide Table, Paddle Slalom, Tray Trek, Cannonball Cup, and the five-phase Big Stand, with the twists named in PLAN.md §5 (breath guide, drifting pose ring and gull, squat tempo, plank flop, 96 BPM two-lane chart, hula-style spin and ring catches, lap with step-rate zone, shell-tilt boards, paddle gates, tray of smoothies, cannonball judges, Long Stillness). Each event has a card, countdown, result (score, stars, deadpan title, minutes, personal best, Retry or Back), a bot that earns at least 2 stars on 5 seeds, and an idle-input result under 1 star. Score events use 0–1000 with stars at 400, 650, and 850; Plank uses the time gates in PLAN.md (25 / 40 / 55 s). Time-cut permission in PLAN.md §17 does not apply: this computer can run the full roster.\\n10→4. **10-foot play shell, audio, and dev tools.** Same screens for pad, keyboard, and mouse. Prompts follow the last input: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs via Settings, keycaps, or mouse buttons. Bindings match PLAN.md §3, including pause and schedule. After 10 seconds (600 sim ticks) with no input on the title or pre-game menus, attract mode loops the PLAN.md §12 sequence with a DEMO ribbon; any input returns to the title; nothing is saved; it stays silent until a real input. Pause freezes the world and opens Dev Mode (not in a hub session) with jump, cheats, time scale, live sliders for the tunables named in PLAN.md §11, and reset to defaults. Settings include music default 0.10 linear, SFX, voices, glyphs, lean sensitivity, wobble assist, rhythm offset, text speed, fullscreen, and reset-save confirm. Touch-only devices get a “Controller or keyboard required” card plus Settings, no touch controls, and attract keeps running. All music, at least 30 sound effects, and Animalese/Simlish voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); speakers are audibly distinct; dialogue types out with syllable blips. UI is large enough for 10 feet (body ≥34 px and titles ≥64 px at 1080p, scaled down with the frame), at most two lines of dialogue, no dense stat tables. Look is high-key pastel on the engine’s cel style, with new beach and wellness props and new exercise poses; library props only for generic dressing.\\n5. **Saves, local multiplayer, and AI Hub.** Versioned `localStorage` saves cover up to four guest slots (name from a preset list, look, day, minutes, stars, bests, ghosts, ages, flip-flops, unlocks) and global settings, written on day change and on each result. Real dates passed in at boot drive absence comments; the sim does not read the clock. Play Together is hot-seat for 2–4 guests through the same three-event medley, with a pass-the-pad card and a podium. Yesterday You replays a best run beside you from day 3 and does not affect scoring. An AI Hub session in mode `ghost`, 1–4 players, skips title, menu, attract, quit, restart, and dev mode, and opens into the Inter-Resort Medley (Paddle Slalom, Tide Table, Cannonball Cup, same seed, same course). Other seats are tinted ghosts in a matching area only. Final score is the sum (0–3000) reported to the hub. Solo play leaves `AIHub.active` false. The About/Credits screen shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback, plus font and motion-library credits. No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features remain.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. **gating:** Add in-repo tests that construct the shipped game from a fresh start (no browser, no GPU) and drive its real update loop. Assert criterion 2’s bot run (3 seeds), criterion 3’s per-event bot and idle results (5 seeds), balance-model settle and tip timing, age direction and 18–99 bounds, attract start at 600 idle ticks plus exit without saving, pause freezing world time, music default 0.10, a dev slider changing a live value and resetting, save round-trip, medley determinism for the same seed and inputs, and Play Together reaching a podium. Lint shipped text for `TODO`, placeholder copy, weight/BMI/diet talk, and Nintendo names outside the About source line. Put them behind one command that prints only failures and a final `N passed, 0 failed` line. Run that command twice; both runs must print the same pass line. Save the log to `{SCRATCH}/tests-core.log`.\\n2. **gating:** Launch the shipped HTML on its real entry path more than once. Prefer `file://`. If a server is required, bind `127.0.0.1` with `python3 -u -m http.server 0` and stop only that PID. Use Playwright Chromium (already installed) with the WebGPU flags in TASK.md. At 320×180, twice: zero page errors, `__gameStatus` appears and its tick advances across samples, a standard-mapping gamepad stub moves menu focus and confirms, keyboard does the same, mouse hover moves focus and click activates using rects the page publishes, prompts change with the device, Settings exposes Xbox glyphs, and after about 10 real seconds attract starts and an input exits it. Phone and tablet Chromium profiles show “Controller or keyboard required”, keep attract running, and a Settings tap opens Settings. WebKit with an iPhone profile (no WebGPU here) shows the fallback that both a controller or keyboard and WebGPU are required. The drawing surface’s dimensions equal the requested viewport. A blank WebGPU canvas while ticks advance is the known software-readback limit, not a pass: also capture a 1920×1080 frame with the engine’s headless capture of the title (menu and title text readable) and of one event (figure plus balance puck substantially filling the frame). A blank engine capture is a failure. Two browser boots and two engine captures must agree. Logs and images go to `{SCRATCH}/`. If Playwright or the engine capture cannot start, save that launcher error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log`; criteria 2–4 then rest on step 1 plus the static checks in step 4. A capture that runs and returns a blank frame is not this fallback.\\n3. **gating:** Hub check on the shipped page. Serve the page with the hub harness files and open the harness for 2 players; for 3 and 4 players with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; and for `scenario=host-leave`. Each run must finish with the harness complete flag and its own pass flag set. Then open the page outside a session, press Enter and Space, and require no errors, `AIHub.active` false, and `Math.random` left unseeded. The session client embedded in the page must byte-match the downloaded `session.js`. Save logs to `{SCRATCH}/tests-hub.log`. If the hub files cannot be fetched, keep this step gating and record the blocker in `{SCRATCH}/hub-unverifiable.log` instead of inventing a pass.\\n4. **gating:** Static hand-in check. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object with the TASK.md keys; `usage` is `added on import`; `aiHub.name` equals the title screen. The HTML filename and dev-folder name match criterion 1. The page’s about-data block includes Source Information and the three feedback lists. `PROGRESS.md` exists. Dev-folder notes contain the 50 titles and the RNG output. Confirm no audio files are referenced by the game.\\n5. **evidence:** One 1920×1080 engine capture per set, plus a short filmstrip of at least one hand-keyed pose. Sound: either the unit render at music 0.10 is about a tenth of full scale, or a browser patch of the audio buffer shows non-silent music after a gesture. Absence of these images does not fail the goal when steps 1–4 pass.\\n\\n20→## Non-goals\\n- Not a Wii Fit recreation: no BMI or weight goals, no Miis or face editors, no Wuhu Island, no source music, layouts, or quoted lines, and not the full 40-activity list (soccer heading, ski jump, hula hoop as named in the source, and the rest).\\n- No real Balance Board, DualSense, iPhone, TV, listener, or second human. Use the stand-ins in TASK.md.\\n- No touch-play controls, no shared-world netcode, no FPS optimization, and no review subagents.\\n- No audio files and no spoken real words.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work root: `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json`, and `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132.html`.\\n- Dev folder of that basename: clone of `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall` (PLAN.md was checked against commit `49ef2ba`), game content and tests, notes, and a small README for adding an event, a set, or a song.\\n- AI Hub client files from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` (`session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`).\\n30→- Fredoka and Nunito (SIL OFL) embedded for UI text; engine puppet, egui, and synthesized audio extended as PLAN.md §§8–14 describe. Behavior is the contract; file names in PLAN.md are guidance.\\n- Playwright 1.63 (system Python, not `python3 -I`), Cargo, and `wasm-bindgen` 0.2.129. Chromium only for WebGPU play; Firefox and WebKit are fallback-message checks.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md for design numbers, writing, and the hub snippet; do not invent a second balance model or a second synth. Keep the whole flow (menus, dialogue, pause, settings, dev toggles, events) in the simulation so tests drive it with no GPU. Keep UI a pure view of that state. Keep tunable numbers and all dialogue in data files the tests load. Build a single HTML only after the sim tests pass. Disable the engine’s demo keys and on-screen chrome while this game is active so captures show the game, not the engine shell. Record any spec gap in one line in the dev notes and continue.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [x] Pick the title with a real RNG from 50 riffs, record the draw, create the dev folder, clone the engine, and keep `PROGRESS.md` current.\\n- [x] Register the game, hide engine chrome, add lean input and the title/pause shell, save, and the hub page tags so a boot smoke test passes.\\n- [x] Implement the shared balance model, the morning check, the island day loop, and one full event; lock them with sim tests.\\n40→- [x] Implement the remaining events, day unlocks, Big Stand, both endings, free play, and the bot playthrough.\\n- [x] Add synthesized music, effects, and gibberish voices (music default 0.10), attract mode, settings, dev sliders with reset, and the touch-only card.\\n- [x] Add wardrobe, flip-flops, Yesterday You, Play Together, and the hub ghost medley; run the hub harness.\\n- [x] Write the full script and About text, fill `exit-interview.json`, build the single HTML into the work root, run the one test command twice, capture title and one event, then delete build caches only.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n- Splash rounds call `AIHub.ready()` when `connect` resolves. Waiting for `__gameStatus` left the fourth software-WebGPU copy past the harness's 20s ready gate. A later `ready()` is a no-op.\\n- Big Stand uses music bed 3 and the credits use bed 6, matching the sim's song map rather than the plan's bed numbers. Long Stillness is song 0, which stops the bed.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n50→- PLAN.md §17 allows cutting Tray Trek, Floatie Spin, and Plank Standoff if time runs short. The objective requires everything this computer can run, so a cut roster fails criterion 3.\\n- TASK.md warns that a headless WebGPU screenshot can be blank even when the frame is live. A blank browser bitmap is not success and not an automatic environment waiver: the engine’s own capture must show a filled frame (step 2). Only a launcher that will not start is the fallback.\\n- Browser timing at 1920×1080 is too slow here (software WebGPU). The 10-second attract check is valid only around 320×180; a slow 1080p browser clock is not a game bug. Judge the 10-foot layout from the 1080p engine capture.\\n- Firefox and WebKit have no WebGPU here, so they cannot play the game. There is no physical pad, touch screen, listener, second human, or TV.\\n- A fresh clone may not be commit `49ef2ba`. If a “verified” engine note is wrong on the cloned revision, adapt and record the commit and the change; do not pretend the old note still held.\\n- Other agents share this machine. Use one browser, bind an OS-picked localhost port, and stop only PIDs this run started. `earlyoom` killing the browser is memory pressure, not a game bug.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-222132__x35112d%2Fwork/01a1aa27-ba86-7730-89c2-9178ff908bce/goal/plan.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"# Plan: Ship a playable Tippy Cay balance-and-wellness demo inspired by Wii Fit, plus the hand-in files TASK.md names\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-in package.** The work-folder root contains `PROGRESS.md`, a parseable `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape TASK.md specifies (every value filled, `\\\"usage\\\"` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.genre` one of the allowed catalogue genres, `aiHub.players` `1–4`), and one self-contained HTML file named `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132.html`. A sibling folder with that same basename (no `.html`) holds the dev tree. `{Title}` is a playful riff on “Wii Fit”, chosen by listing 50 titles and selecting one with a real RNG; the same string is the title-screen name, the page `<title>`, and `aiHub.name`. The 50 titles and the RNG draw are recorded in the dev folder. Opening the HTML from disk boots this game (a WebGPU-missing message only when WebGPU is absent).\\n2. **Wii Fit loop, original world.** It is a third-person sports mini-game anthology with a light life-sim frame: a five-day stay at Tippy Cay staffed by talking gym gear. The only play verb is a centre-of-pressure lean (left stick, keyboard ramp, or mouse offset from screen centre — last device wins) driving one shared balance model: shaped lean, stiffness/damping, sim-time wobble, tip only after the dot stays outside the unit disc for the tip time. Tips cost time or score and never end the stay. Each morning a wobble check (still, lean targets, eyes-closed tone, step timing) yields a Balance Age from PLAN.md’s skill formula, clamped 18–99; better input gives a lower age. There is no weight, BMI, body-shape, or diet content, and no Nintendo product names inside play (About source line and the title riff only). Days open in order — stillness, strength, cardio, lagoon — with minute goals 3, 5, 7, 9, then a Day-5 Big Stand whose last phase is 30 seconds of doing nothing (fake “press to skip”; any press forfeits the bonus). First clear of an event banks 1 + stars; repeats bank 1. Age 40 or better may check out by rowboat; otherwise the Extended Stay ending. Both roll credits and unlock free play. A headless bot, from a fresh New Stay, reaches credits on 3 seeds: won, 5 stamps, every event played, under 60 sim-minutes, without reading the wall clock.\\n3. **Every event is a finished mini-game.** Shipped and directly startable: Morning Wobble Check, Breath Bloom, Pose Parade, Tempo Squats, Plank Standoff, Step Party, Floatie Spin, Morning Lap, Tide Table, Paddle Slalom, Tray Trek, Cannonball Cup, and the five-phase Big Stand, with the twists named in PLAN.md §5 (breath guide, drifting pose ring and gull, squat tempo, plank flop, 96 BPM two-lane chart, hula-style spin and ring catches, lap with step-rate zone, shell-tilt boards, paddle gates, tray of smoothies, cannonball judges, Long Stillness). Each event has a card, countdown, result (score, stars, deadpan title, minutes, personal best, Retry or Back), a bot that earns at least 2 stars on 5 seeds, and an idle-input result under 1 star. Score events use 0–1000 with stars at 400, 650, and 850; Plank uses the time gates in PLAN.md (25 / 40 / 55 s). Time-cut permission in PLAN.md §17 does not apply: this computer can run the full roster.\\n4. **10-foot play shell, audio, and dev tools.** Same screens for pad, keyboard, and mouse. Prompts follow the last input: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs via Settings, keycaps, or mouse buttons. Bindings match PLAN.md §3, including pause and schedule. After 10 seconds (600 sim ticks) with no input on the title or pre-game menus, attract mode loops the PLAN.md §12 sequence with a DEMO ribbon; any input returns to the title; nothing is saved; it stays silent until a real input. Pause freezes the world and opens Dev Mode (not in a hub session) with jump, cheats, time scale, live sliders for the tunables named in PLAN.md §11, and reset to defaults. Settings include music default 0.10 linear, SFX, voices, glyphs, lean sensitivity, wobble assist, rhythm offset, text speed, fullscreen, and reset-save confirm. Touch-only devices get a “Controller or keyboard required” card plus Settings, no touch controls, and attract keeps running. All music, at least 30 sound effects, and Animalese/Simlish voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); speakers are audibly distinct; dialogue types out with syllable blips. UI is large enough for 10 feet (body ≥34 px and titles ≥64 px at 1080p, scaled down with the frame), at most two lines of dialogue, no dense stat tables. Look is high-key pastel on the engine’s cel style, with new beach and wellness props and new exercise poses; library props only for generic dressing.\\n5. **Saves, local multiplayer, and AI Hub.** Versioned `localStorage` saves cover up to four guest slots (name from a preset list, look, day, minutes, stars, bests, ghosts, ages, flip-flops, unlocks) and global settings, written on day change and on each result. Real dates passed in at boot drive absence comments; the sim does not read the clock. Play Together is hot-seat for 2–4 guests through the same three-event medley, with a pass-the-pad card and a podium. Yesterday You replays a best run beside you from day 3 and does not affect scoring. An AI Hub session in mode `ghost`, 1–4 players, skips title, menu, attract, quit, restart, and dev mode, and opens into the Inter-Resort Medley (Paddle Slalom, Tide Table, Cannonball Cup, same seed, same course). Other seats are tinted ghosts in a matching area only. Final score is the sum (0–3000) reported to the hub. Solo play leaves `AIHub.active` false. The About/Credits screen shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback, plus font and motion-library credits. No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features remain.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. **gating:** Add in-repo tests that construct the shipped game from a fresh start (no browser, no GPU) and drive its real update loop. Assert criterion 2’s bot run (3 seeds), criterion 3’s per-event bot and idle results (5 seeds), balance-model settle and tip timing, age direction and 18–99 bounds, attract start at 600 idle ticks plus exit without saving, pause freezing world time, music default 0.10, a dev slider changing a live value and resetting, save round-trip, medley determinism for the same seed and inputs, and Play Together reaching a podium. Lint shipped text for `TODO`, placeholder copy, weight/BMI/diet talk, and Nintendo names outside the About source line. Put them behind one command that prints only failures and a final `N passed, 0 failed` line. Run that command twice; both runs must print the same pass line. Save the log to `{SCRATCH}/tests-core.log`.\\n2. **gating:** Launch the shipped HTML on its real entry path more than once. Prefer `file://`. If a server is required, bind `127.0.0.1` with `python3 -u -m http.server 0` and stop only that PID. Use Playwright Chromium (already installed) with the WebGPU flags in TASK.md. At 320×180, twice: zero page errors, `__gameStatus` appears and its tick advances across samples, a standard-mapping gamepad stub moves menu focus and confirms, keyboard does the same, mouse hover moves focus and click activates using rects the page publishes, prompts change with the device, Settings exposes Xbox glyphs, and after about 10 real seconds attract starts and an input exits it. Phone and tablet Chromium profiles show “Controller or keyboard required”, keep attract running, and a Settings tap opens Settings. WebKit with an iPhone profile (no WebGPU here) shows the fallback that both a controller or keyboard and WebGPU are required. The drawing surface’s dimensions equal the requested viewport. A blank WebGPU canvas while ticks advance is the known software-readback limit, not a pass: also capture a 1920×1080 frame with the engine’s headless capture of the title (menu and title text readable) and of one event (figure plus balance puck substantially filling the frame). A blank engine capture is a failure. Two browser boots and two engine captures must agree. Logs and images go to `{SCRATCH}/`. If Playwright or the engine capture cannot start, save that launcher error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log`; criteria 2–4 then rest on step 1 plus the static checks in step 4. A capture that runs and returns a blank frame is not this fallback.\\n3. **gating:** Hub check on the shipped page. Serve the page with the hub harness files and open the harness for 2 players; for 3 and 4 players with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; and for `scenario=host-leave`. Each run must finish with the harness complete flag and its own pass flag set. Then open the page outside a session, press Enter and Space, and require no errors, `AIHub.active` false, and `Math.random` left unseeded. The session client embedded in the page must byte-match the downloaded `session.js`. Save logs to `{SCRATCH}/tests-hub.log`. If the hub files cannot be fetched, keep this step gating and record the blocker in `{SCRATCH}/hub-unverifiable.log` instead of inventing a pass.\\n4. **gating:** Static hand-in check. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object with the TASK.md keys; `usage` is `added on import`; `aiHub.name` equals the title screen. The HTML filename and dev-folder name match criterion 1. The page’s about-data block includes Source Information and the three feedback lists. `PROGRESS.md` exists. Dev-folder notes contain the 50 titles and the RNG output. Confirm no audio files are referenced by the game.\\n5. **evidence:** One 1920×1080 engine capture per set, plus a short filmstrip of at least one hand-keyed pose. Sound: either the unit render at music 0.10 is about a tenth of full scale, or a browser patch of the audio buffer shows non-silent music after a gesture. Absence of these images does not fail the goal when steps 1–4 pass.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- Not a Wii Fit recreation: no BMI or weight goals, no Miis or face editors, no Wuhu Island, no source music, layouts, or quoted lines, and not the full 40-activity list (soccer heading, ski jump, hula hoop as named in the source, and the rest).\\n- No real Balance Board, DualSense, iPhone, TV, listener, or second human. Use the stand-ins in TASK.md.\\n- No touch-play controls, no shared-world netcode, no FPS optimization, and no review subagents.\\n- No audio files and no spoken real words.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work root: `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json`, and `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-222132.html`.\\n- Dev folder of that basename: clone of `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall` (PLAN.md was checked against commit `49ef2ba`), game content and tests, notes, and a small README for adding an event, a set, or a song.\\n- AI Hub client files from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` (`session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`).\\n- Fredoka and Nunito (SIL OFL) embedded for UI text; engine puppet, egui, and synthesized audio extended as PLAN.md §§8–14 describe. Behavior is the contract; file names in PLAN.md are guidance.\\n- Playwright 1.63 (system Python, not `python3 -I`), Cargo, and `wasm-bindgen` 0.2.129. Chromium only for WebGPU play; Firefox and WebKit are fallback-message checks.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md for design numbers, writing, and the hub snippet; do not invent a second balance model or a second synth. Keep the whole flow (menus, dialogue, pause, settings, dev toggles, events) in the simulation so tests drive it with no GPU. Keep UI a pure view of that state. Keep tunable numbers and all dialogue in data files the tests load. Build a single HTML only after the sim tests pass. Disable the engine’s demo keys and on-screen chrome while this game is active so captures show the game, not the engine shell. Record any spec gap in one line in the dev notes and continue.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [x] Pick the title with a real RNG from 50 riffs, record the draw, create the dev folder, clone the engine, and keep `PROGRESS.md` current.\\n- [x] Register the game, hide engine chrome, add lean input and the title/pause shell, save, and the hub page tags so a boot smoke test passes.\\n- [x] Implement the shared balance model, the morning check, the island day loop, and one full event; lock them with sim tests.\\n- [x] Implement the remaining events, day unlocks, Big Stand, both endings, free play, and the bot playthrough.\\n- [x] Add synthesized music, effects, and gibberish voices (music default 0.10), attract mode, settings, dev sliders with reset, and the touch-only card.\\n- [x] Add wardrobe, flip-flops, Yesterday You, Play Together, and the hub ghost medley; run the hub harness.\\n- [x] Write the full script and About text, fill `exit-interview.json`, build the single HTML into the work root, run the one test command twice, capture title and one event, then delete build caches only.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n- Splash rounds call `AIHub.ready()` when `connect` resolves. Waiting for `__gameStatus` left the fourth software-WebGPU copy past the harness's 20s ready gate. A later `ready()` is a no-op.\\n- Big Stand uses music bed 3 and the credits use bed 6, matching the sim's song map rather than the plan's bed numbers. Long Stillness is song 0, which stops the bed.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md §17 allows cutting Tray Trek, Floatie Spin, and Plank Standoff if time runs short. The objective requires everything this computer can run, so a cut roster fails criterion 3.\\n- TASK.md warns that a headless WebGPU screenshot can be blank even when the frame is live. A blank browser bitmap is not success and not an automatic environment waiver: the engine’s own capture must show a filled frame (step 2). Only a launcher that will not start is the fallback.\\n- Browser timing at 1920×1080 is too slow here (software WebGPU). The 10-second attract check is valid only around 320×180; a slow 1080p browser clock is not a game bug. Judge the 10-foot layout from the 1080p engine capture.\\n- Firefox and WebKit have no WebGPU here, so they cannot play the game. There is no physical pad, touch screen, listener, second human, or TV.\\n- A fresh clone may not be commit `49ef2ba`. If a “verified” engine note is wrong on the cloned revision, adapt and record the commit and the change; do not pretend the old note still held.\\n- Other agents share this machine. Use one browser, bind an OS-picked localhost port, and stop only PIDs this run started. `earlyoom` killing the browser is memory pressure, not a game bug.\\n\",\"total_lines\":55}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-0e63656b-9649-4d25-9188-32fb0810cd03-4","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,50,49,51,50,95,95,120,51,53,49,49,50,100,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,54,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,50,49,51,50,95,95,120,51,53,49,49,50,100,47,119,111,114,107,47,87,111,98,98,108,101,32,77,105,110,117,116,101,115,45,87,105,105,32,70,105,116,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,50,49,51,50,47,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,99,114,97,116,101,115,47,112,97,118,95,99,111,114,101,47,115,114,99,47,102,105,116,95,117,105,46,114,115,10,51,53,58,32,32,32,32,47,47,47,32,70,117,108,108,45,115,99,114,101,101,110,32,100,105,109,44,32,48,32,99,108,101,97,114,44,32,49,32,98,108,97,99,107,46,32,84,104,101,32,101,121,101,115,45,99,108,111,115,101,100,32,99,104,101,99,107,32,117,115,101,115,32,116,104,105,115,46,10,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,50,49,51,50,95,95,120,51,53,